    "created_at": "2026-10-25T11:14:34Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-25T11:14:34Z",
    "body": "[While working on improving `getFile()` on Firefox](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2051927), we faced this issue: IMO the spec is too vague today, and would benefit from some clarification, as @annevk noted.\n\nFirst: as noted in the first comment, it should be clarified a copy is not expected.\nMaybe it could be replaced by something like: \"_Set f’s underlying byte sequence to entry’s [binary data](https://fs.spec.whatwg.org/#file-entry-binary-data)_\", i.e. removing the copy part.\n\nSecond: if a copy is indeed not expected, the non-normative text above should be made normative somehow to avoid web-compat issues: \"If the file on disk changes or is removed after this method is called, the returned File object will likely be no longer readable\" also implies the handle shall be invalidated after the file has been modified (whether it's from an external process or from another handle in the browser).\nI think that part comes from the FileAPI spec, which can throw an exception if \"the snapshot state may have changed. See NotReadableError.\" (Since getFile() returns a File (step 4 \"_Let f be a new File._\")).\n\nThe \"snapshot state\" wording + the \"copy the underlying bytes\" could confuse someone in thinking this is a function to get snapshots of the file, while it's not.\n\nAnd this is not what browsers implement today.\n\nHaving real snapshots could be a valid usecase and interesting feature for web dev though, especially if made efficient if implemented as copy on write, so enforcing this behavior could be an option too.\n\nI made some WPT tests (web-platform-tests/wpt#64190) to check what browser are doing today, and ran it (on macOS only).\nAttaching the result here. All browsers today implement invalidation of the file most of the time, though the results are inconsistent.\n\n[getfile-results-2.html](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/33924210/getfile-results-2.html)",
    "author_association": "NONE",
